There are many ways to compute $\cos 72^\circ.$ Here is one way using the identity $\cos (A + B) = \cos A \cos B - \sin A \sin B.$ Let $A = 36^\circ$ and $B = 36^\circ.$ Then $\cos (36^\circ + 36^\circ) = \cos 72^\circ.$

Using the formula:
\begin{align*}
\cos (36^\circ + 36^\circ) = \cos 36^\circ \cos 36^\circ - \sin 36^\circ \sin 36^\circ &= \cos^2 36^\circ - \sin^2 36^\circ.
\end{align*}
Since $\cos^2 36^\circ + \sin^2 36^\circ = 1,$
\begin{align*}
\cos^2 36^\circ - \sin^2 36^\circ &= \cos^2 36^\circ - (1 - \cos^2 36^\circ)\\
&= 2\cos^2 36^\circ - 1.
\end{align*}
Substituting $\cos^2 36^\circ = (1 + \sqrt{5})/4,$ we have
\begin{align*}
\cos 72^\circ &= 2 \cdot \left( \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{4} \right) - 1\\
&= \frac{\sqrt{5} + 1}{2} - 1\\
&= \frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{2}.
\end{align*}

