\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) &= p(\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})) + q(1) + r(\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}))\\
1 &= 0 + q + r(0)\\
1 &= q\\
\end{align*}

Solving for $r$ from the equation $(\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})) = 0$:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) &= 0\\
\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) &= p(\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})) + q(1) + r(\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}))\\
0 &= p(0) + q(1) + r(0)\\
0 &= q\\
\end{align*}

Substituting the values of $p$, $q$, and $r$ into the equation:
\begin{align*}
1 &= p(0) + 1(1) + r(0)\\
1 &= 1\\
\end{align*}

Since $q = 1$ and $p + q + r = 1$, we must have $p = 0$ and $r = 0$. Therefore, $p + q + r = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1$.

