In $\triangle ABC$ points $D$ and $E$ lie on $\overline{BC}$ and $\overline{AC}$, respectively.  If $\overline{AD}$ and $\overline{BE}$ intersect at $T$ so that $AT/DT=3$ and $BT/ET=4$, what is $CD/BD$?

[asy]
pair A,B,C,D,I,T;
A=(0,0);
B=(6,8);
C=(11,0);
D=(9.33,2.66);
I=(7.5,0);
T=(6.5,2);
label("$T$",T,NW);
label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$E$",I,S);
label("$A$",A,S);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$B$",B,N);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--I,linewidth(0.7));
[/asy]

initial_response:
To find CD/BD, we first need to express the ratios AT/DT and BT/ET in terms of the side lengths. Using similar triangles, we get:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{AT}{DT} &= \frac{AC}{TC} \\
    \frac{BT}{ET} &= \frac{BC}{TC} \\
\end{align*}
From the first equation, we get:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{AC}{TC} &= 3 \\
    TC &= \frac{AC}{3} \\
\end{align*}
From the second equation, we get:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{BC}{TC} &= 4 \\
    TC &= \frac{BC}{4} \\
\end{align*}
Since TC must be the same for both equations, we can equate the two expressions:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{AC}{3} &= \frac{BC}{4} \\
    \frac{AC}{BC} &= \frac{3}{4} \\
    \frac{AC}{BC} &= \frac{CD}{BC-D} \\
    \frac{AC}{BC} &= \frac{CD}{BD-CD} \\
    \frac{AC}{BC} &= \frac{CD}{BD} \\
    \frac{3}{4} &= \frac{CD}{BD} \\
\end{align*}
Therefore, CD/BD = 3/4.

