The original equation sin^2 x + sin^2 2x + sin^2 3x + sin^2 4x = 2 can be transformed into the equivalent equation \cos ax \cos bx \cos cx = 0 by utilizing sum-to-product trigonometric identities.

The key observation is to apply sum-to-product identities for cosine:

\begin{align*}
\cos 2x + \cos 4x &= 2\cos 3x\cos x\\
\cos 6x + \cos 8x &= 2\cos 7x\cos x
\end{align*}
Substituting these into the original equation results in:
\begin{align*}
\cos 2x + \cos 4x + \cos 6x + \cos 8x &= 0\\
2\cos 3x\cos x + 2\cos 7x\cos x &= 0\\
2\cos x(\cos 3x + \cos 7x) &= 0
\end{align*}

By applying sum-to-product identity again:
\begin{align*}
\cos 3x + \cos 7x &= 2\cos 5x\cos 2x\\
2\cos x(\cos 5x\cos 2x) &= 0
\end{align*}
This gives us the desired form \cos ax \cos bx \cos cx = 0.

Therefore, a = 5, b = 1, c = 2.

The final answer is: a + b + c = 5 + 1 + 2 = 8.

