The limit of (sin x)/x as x approaches 0 is a well-known standard limit in calculus. It equals 1 and is often considered a foundational identity.

This can be justified using the squeeze theorem or a Taylor expansion of sin x:
sin x ≈ x - x³/6 + ... ⇒ (sin x)/x ≈ 1 - x²/6 + ... → 1 as x → 0.

So, lim x→0 (sin x)/x = 1.

The integral of 1/x from -1 to 1 is not defined in the usual sense because the function 1/x has a discontinuity at x = 0. Since the integrand is not defined at x = 0, and the limits of integration span across this discontinuity, the integral is considered improper. When evaluated as a limit from both sides, the integral diverges, meaning the area under the curve from -1 to 1 does not converge to a finite value.

