We want to find the maximum value of $f(x) = 10^x - 10^{2x}.$ To do this, we first find the critical points of $f(x).$
Let's rewrite $f(x)$ as $g(x) = 10^x - 10^{2x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get $g'(x) = 10^x \ln 10 - 2 \cdot 10^{2x} \ln 10.$ Setting the derivative equal to 0, we get $g'(x) = 10^x \ln 10 (1 - 2 \cdot 10^x) = 0.$ Since $10^x \ln 10 > 0$ for all $x,$ the only critical point is when $1 - 2 \cdot 10^x = 0,$ giving us $10^x = \frac{1}{2}.$ This critical point is a maximum since the Hessian matrix $H_g(x)$ is negative semi-definite at $x = \log_{10} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right).$ In fact, we have that
