revised_response:
Let $\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 5 & d \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = k \mathbf{A}$ for some constant $k.$

The formula for the inverse of a $2\times2$ matrix is $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(\mathbf{A})} \begin{pmatrix} d & -3 \\ -5 & 2 \end{pmatrix}.$
Since $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = k \mathbf{A},$ we also have:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2d-15} \begin{pmatrix} d & -3 \\ -5 & 2 \end{pmatrix}
&= k \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 5 & d \end{pmatrix} \\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{d}{2d-15} & = 2k \\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{-3}{2d-15} & = 3k \\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{-5}{2d-15} & = 5k \\
\end{align*}
Since these three equations must be simultaneously satisfied, we can solve for $d$ and $k.$

Multiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding to the second equation yields: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{2d-15}=-2k & \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad -\frac{3}{2d-15}=3k\\
& \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad -3 = (3k)(2d-15)\\
& \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad \frac{-3}{2d-15}=3k\\
\end{align*}

Multiplying the equation by $-5$ and adding to the third equation yields: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{2d-15}=2k & \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad \frac{-5}{2d-15}=5k\\
& \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad -5 = (5k)(2d-15)\\
& \Rightarrow\qquad\qquad \frac{-5}{2d-15}=5k\\
\end{align*}

Subtracting the first equation from the second equation yields: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{-5}{2d-15} - \frac{d}{2d-15} & = 5k - 2k \\
\Rightarrow\qquad\qquad\qquad \frac{-5-d}{2d-15} & = 3k\\
\end{align*}

Equating the two expressions for $3k$ yields: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{-5-d}{2d-15} & = \frac{-3}{2d-15}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad\qquad\qquad -3 & = -3 - d\\
\Rightarrow\qquad\qquad\qquad d & = \boxed{5}.
\end{align*}
Substituting $d = 5$ into the equation $\frac{d}{2d-15} = 2k$ yields: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{5}{45} & = 2k\\
\Rightarrow\qquad\qquad\qquad k & = \frac{1}{9}.
\end{align*}
So $(d,k) = \boxed{(5,\frac{1}{9})}.$

