revised_response:
By the property of the inverse of a product, $(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1},$ it follows that $(\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2.$
To calculate $(\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2,$ we square $\mathbf{A}^{-1}.$
\begin{align*}
\left(\begin{pmatrix} -4 & 1 \\ 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\right)^2 &= \left(\begin{pmatrix} (-4)^2 + 0 & (-4)(1) + 2 \\ 0 & 2^2 \end{pmatrix}\right) \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 16 & -2 \\ 0 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}Note that this property is a basic result of linear algebra, often stated as: If $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{B}$ are invertible matrices, then $(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1}.$

